/-
# Minimal base size versus independent sets (Kourovka Notebook Problem 16.45)

Kourovka Notebook, Problem 16.45 (P. J. Cameron): is `b(G) = μ'(G)` for every finite group `G`,
where `b(G)` is the largest size of a minimal base over all permutation representations of `G` and
`μ'(G)` is the largest size of an independent set in `G`?
-/

module

public import Batteries.Util.ProofWanted
public import Mathlib.Algebra.Group.Subgroup.Lattice
public import Mathlib.Order.Lattice.Nat

@[expose] public section

namespace MathlibExt.GroupTheory.MinimalBaseIndependentSetKourovka16Problem45Wanted

/-!
Source: E. I. Khukhro and V. D. Mazurov (eds.), Unsolved Problems in Group Theory: The Kourovka
Notebook, 21st issue, arXiv:1401.0300v49 (2026), Problem 16.45 (P. J. Cameron). UnsolvedMath record
UM-00701237 (`KOU-16.45`), dataset ulamai/UnsolvedMath v1.8.0.

Permutation representations are arbitrary actions of `G` on a type `Ω` in `Type`; a base exists only
for a faithful action. A base is a finite set of points, since a minimal base never repeats a point.
Both maxima are `sSup` of sets of naturals that are bounded for finite `G` (by `|G|`), so they are
the actual maxima.
-/

/-- **Base of a group action.** For a group $G$ acting on a set $\Omega$, a finite set
$B \subseteq \Omega$ is a base when its pointwise stabilizer is trivial: the only $g \in G$ with
$g \cdot \omega = \omega$ for all $\omega \in B$ is $g = 1$. -/
def IsBase (G Ω : Type) [Group G] [MulAction G Ω] (B : Finset Ω) : Prop :=
  ∀ g : G, (∀ ω ∈ B, g • ω = ω) → g = 1

open Classical in
/-- **Minimal base.** For a group $G$ acting on a set $\Omega$, a finite set $B \subseteq \Omega$
is a minimal base when it is a base, that is, its pointwise stabilizer in $G$ is trivial, and for
every $\omega \in B$ the set $B \setminus \{\omega\}$ is not a base. -/
def IsMinimalBase (G Ω : Type) [Group G] [MulAction G Ω] (B : Finset Ω) : Prop :=
  IsBase G Ω B ∧ ∀ ω ∈ B, ¬ IsBase G Ω (B.erase ω)

/-- **Maximal size of a minimal base.** For a group $G$, the number $b(G)$ is the supremum in
$\mathbb{N}$ of the sizes of the minimal bases of $G$ over all actions of $G$ on a set $\Omega$,
where a minimal base is a finite set $B \subseteq \Omega$ whose pointwise stabilizer in $G$ is
trivial while that of $B \setminus \{\omega\}$ is not, for every $\omega \in B$. It is the largest
such size when these sizes are bounded, as they are for finite $G$, and $0$ when they are
unbounded. -/
noncomputable def maxMinimalBaseSize (G : Type) [Group G] : ℕ :=
  sSup {k | ∃ (Ω : Type) (_ : MulAction G Ω) (B : Finset Ω), IsMinimalBase G Ω B ∧ B.card = k}

open Classical in
/-- **Independent subset of a group.** A finite subset $S$ of a group $G$ is independent when no
element $s \in S$ lies in the subgroup of $G$ generated by $S \setminus \{s\}$. -/
def IsIndependent {G : Type} [Group G] (S : Finset G) : Prop :=
  ∀ s ∈ S, s ∉ Subgroup.closure ((S.erase s : Finset G) : Set G)

/-- **Maximal size of an independent set.** For a group $G$, a finite subset $S \subseteq G$ is
independent when no $s \in S$ lies in the subgroup generated by $S \setminus \{s\}$. The number
$\mu'(G)$ is the supremum in $\mathbb{N}$ of the sizes of the finite independent subsets of $G$:
their largest size when these sizes are bounded, as they are for finite $G$, and $0$ when they are
unbounded. -/
noncomputable def maxIndependentSize (G : Type) [Group G] : ℕ :=
  sSup {k | ∃ S : Finset G, IsIndependent S ∧ S.card = k}

/-- **Cameron's question on minimal bases and independent sets.** The statement that
$b(G) = \mu'(G)$ for every finite group $G$, where $b(G)$ is the largest size of a minimal base of
$G$ over all actions of $G$ on a set, and $\mu'(G)$ is the largest size of an independent subset
of $G$ (a subset none of whose elements lies in the subgroup generated by the others).

This is Kourovka Notebook Problem 16.45, posed by P. J. Cameron. -/
def maxMinimalBaseSizeEqMaxIndependentSize : Prop :=
  ∀ (G : Type) [Group G] [Finite G], maxMinimalBaseSize G = maxIndependentSize G

/--
**Kourovka Problem 16.45 has a negative answer.** It is not true that $b(G) = \mu'(G)$ for every
finite group $G$, where $b(G)$ is the largest size of a minimal base of $G$ over all actions of $G$
on a set, and $\mu'(G)$ is the largest size of an independent subset of $G$.

The counterexample in the source is Kriebel's affine group $G = \mathbb{F}_{29}^2 \rtimes
\mathrm{SL}_2(5)$ of order $100920$, for which $b(G) = 3 < 4 = \mu'(G)$.

Source: E. I.
Khukhro and V. D. Mazurov (eds.), Unsolved Problems in Group Theory: The Kourovka Notebook, 21st
issue, arXiv:1401.0300v49 (2026), Problem 16.45 (P. J. Cameron); resolution: A. Kriebel, A
counterexample to Kourovka Notebook Problem 16.45, preprint, Zenodo record 22929486 (2026), main
theorem: G = F_29^2 ⋊ SL_2(5) of order 100920 has b(G) = 3 < 4 = μ'(G); the Kourovka Notebook (21st
issue) marks 16.45 solved (AI solution confirmed by P. J. Cameron),
https://zenodo.org/records/22929486.
-/
public theorem_wanted maxMinimalBaseSizeEqMaxIndependentSize_refuted :
    ¬ maxMinimalBaseSizeEqMaxIndependentSize

end MathlibExt.GroupTheory.MinimalBaseIndependentSetKourovka16Problem45Wanted
